<P>
<H1><A name=3DSECTION004000000000000000000></A><A =
name=3Dfeature></A><BR>2. Python=20
-- Feature by Feature </H1>
<P>
<H1><A name=3DSECTION004100000000000000000>2.1 Interactive Python</A> =
</H1>
<P>If you execute Python from the command line with no script, Python =
gives you=20
an interactive prompt. This is an excellent facility for learning Python =
and for=20
trying small snippets of code. Many of the examples that follow were =
developed=20
using the Python interactive prompt.=20
<P>In addition, there are tools that will give you a more powerful and =
fancy=20
Python interactive mode. One example is IPython, which is available at =
<A=20
class=3Durl =
href=3D"http://ipython.scipy.org/">http://ipython.scipy.org/</A>. You=20
may also want to consider using IDLE. IDLE is a graphical integrated =
development=20
environment for Python; it contains a Python shell. You will find a =
script to=20
start up IDLE in the <SPAN class=3Dfile>Tools/scripts</SPAN> directory =
of your=20
Python distribution. IDLE requires Tkinter.=20
<P>
<H1><A name=3DSECTION004200000000000000000>2.2 Data Types</A> </H1>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004210000000000000000>2.2.1 Strings</A> </H2>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004211000000000000000>2.2.1.1 What</A> </H3>
<P>In Python, strings are immutable sequences of characters. They are =
immutable=20
in that in order to modify a string, you must produce a new string.=20
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004212000000000000000>2.2.1.2 When</A> </H3>
<P>Any text information.=20
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004213000000000000000>2.2.1.3 How</A> </H3>
<P>Create a new string from a constant:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>s1 =3D 'abce'
s2 =3D "xyz"
s3 =3D """A
multi-line
string.
"""
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Use any of the string methods, for example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; 'The happy cat ran =
home.'.upper()
'THE HAPPY CAT RAN HOME.'
&gt;&gt;&gt; 'The happy cat ran home.'.find('cat')
10
&gt;&gt;&gt; 'The happy cat ran home.'.find('kitten')
-1
&gt;&gt;&gt; 'The happy cat ran home.'.replace('cat', 'dog')
'The happy dog ran home.'
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Type "<TT class=3Dsamp>help(str)</TT>" or see <A class=3Durl=20
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/string-methods.html">http:/=
/www.python.org/doc/current/lib/string-methods.html</A>=20
for more information on string methods.=20
<P>You can also use the equivalent functions from the <TT=20
class=3Dmodule>string</TT> module. For example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; import string
&gt;&gt;&gt; s1 =3D 'The happy cat ran home.'
&gt;&gt;&gt; string.find(s1, 'happy')
4
</PRE></DIV>
<P>See <A class=3Durl=20
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/module-string.html">http://=
www.python.org/doc/current/lib/module-string.html</A>for=20
more information on the string module.=20
<P>There is also a string formatting operator: "<TT =
class=3Dsamp>%</TT>".=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; state =3D 'California'
&gt;&gt;&gt; 'It never rains in sunny %s.' % state
'It never rains in sunny California.'
</PRE></DIV>
<P>You can use any of the following formatting characters:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3Drealtable>
  <THEAD>
  <TR>
    <TH class=3Dcenter>Conversion</TH>
    <TH class=3Dleft>Meaning</TH>
    <TH class=3Dcenter>Notes</TH></TR></THEAD>
  <TBODY>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>d</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Signed integer decimal.</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>i</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Signed integer decimal.</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>o</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Unsigned octal.</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter>(1)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>u</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Unsigned decimal.</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>x</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Unsigned hexidecimal (lowercase).</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter>(2)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>X</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Unsigned hexidecimal (uppercase).</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter>(2)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>e</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Floating point exponential format (lowercase).</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>E</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Floating point exponential format (uppercase).</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>f</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Floating point decimal format.</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>F</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Floating point decimal format.</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>g</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Same as "<TT class=3Dcharacter>e</TT>" if exponent =
is greater=20
      than -4 or less than precision, "<TT class=3Dcharacter>f</TT>" =
otherwise.</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>G</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Same as "<TT class=3Dcharacter>E</TT>" if exponent =
is greater=20
      than -4 or less than precision, "<TT class=3Dcharacter>F</TT>" =
otherwise.</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>c</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>Single character (accepts integer or single =
character=20
      string).</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>r</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>String (converts any python object using <TT=20
      class=3Dfunction>repr()</TT>).</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter>(3)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>s</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>String (converts any python object using <TT=20
      class=3Dfunction>str()</TT>).</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter>(4)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>%</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>No argument is converted, results in a "<TT=20
      class=3Dcharacter>%</TT>" character in the result.</TD>
    <TD class=3Dcenter></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV>
<P>And these flags:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3Drealtable>
  <THEAD>
  <TR>
    <TH class=3Dcenter>Flag</TH>
    <TH class=3Dleft>Meaning</TH></TR></THEAD>
  <TBODY>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>#</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>The value conversion will use the ``alternate =
form'' (where=20
      defined below).</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>0</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>The conversion will be zero padded for numeric=20
values.</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>-</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>The converted value is left adjusted (overrides the =
"<TT=20
      class=3Dcharacter>0</TT>" conversion if both are given).</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>&nbsp;</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>(a space) A blank should be left before a positive =
number=20
      (or empty string) produced by a signed conversion.</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD class=3Dcenter vAlign=3Dbaseline><TT =
class=3Dcharacter>+</TT></TD>
    <TD class=3Dleft>A sign character ("<TT class=3Dcharacter>+</TT>" or =
"<TT=20
      class=3Dcharacter>-</TT>") will precede the conversion (overrides =
a "space"=20
      flag).</TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV>
<P>See <A class=3Durl=20
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/typesseq-strings.html">http=
://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/typesseq-strings.html</A>for=20
more information on string formatting.=20
<P>You can also write strings to a file and read them from a file. Here =
are some=20
examples:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Writing - For example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; outfile =3D file('tmp.txt', =
'w')
&gt;&gt;&gt; outfile.write('This is line #1\n')
&gt;&gt;&gt; outfile.write('This is line #2\n')
&gt;&gt;&gt; outfile.write('This is line #3\n')
&gt;&gt;&gt; outfile.close()
</PRE></DIV>
  <P>Notes:=20
  <P>
  <UL>
    <LI>Note the end-of-line character at the end of each string.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI>The <TT class=3Dfunction>file</TT> constructor creates a file =
object. It=20
    takes as arguments (1) the file name and (2) a mode. Commonly used =
modes are=20
    "<TT class=3Dsamp>r</TT>" (read), "<TT class=3Dsamp>w</TT>" (write), =
and "<TT=20
    class=3Dsamp>a</TT>"(append). See <A class=3Durl=20
    =
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/built-in-funcs.html">http:/=
/www.python.org/doc/current/lib/built-in-funcs.html</A>for=20
    more modes and more on <TT class=3Dfunction>file</TT>.=20
    <P></P></LI></UL>
  <P></P>
  <LI>Reading an entire file:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; infile =3D file('tmp.txt', =
'r')
&gt;&gt;&gt; content =3D infile.read()
&gt;&gt;&gt; print content
This is line #1
This is line #2
This is line #3

&gt;&gt;&gt; infile.close()
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>Reading a file one line at a time:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; infile =3D file('tmp.txt', =
'r')
&gt;&gt;&gt; for line in infile.readlines():
...     print 'Line:', line
...
Line: This is line #1

Line: This is line #2

Line: This is line #3

&gt;&gt;&gt; infile.close()
</PRE></DIV>
  <P>Notes:=20
  <P>
  <UL>
    <LI>"<TT class=3Dsamp>infile.readlines()</TT>" returns a list of =
lines in the=20
    file. For large files use the file object itself or "<TT=20
    class=3Dsamp>infile.xreadlines()</TT>", both of which are iterators =
for the=20
    lines in the file.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI>Newer Pythons allow you to use infile (or iter(infile)) in the =
same way=20
    that you could use infile.xreadlines() in earlier versions of =
Python. In=20
    effect, the file object is an iterable and produces an iterator. For =

    example:=20
    <P>
    <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; infile =3D file('tmp.txt', =
'r')
&gt;&gt;&gt; for line in infile:
...     print 'Line:', line
...
</PRE></DIV>
    <P></P></LI></UL>
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>A few additional comments about strings:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>A string is a special kind of sequence. So, you can index into the =

  characters of a string and you can iterate over the characters in a =
string.=20
  For example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; s1 =3D 'abcd'
&gt;&gt;&gt; s1[1]
'b'
&gt;&gt;&gt; s1[2]
'c'
&gt;&gt;&gt; for ch in s1:
...   print ch
...
a
b
c
d
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>If you need to do fast or complex string searches, there is a =
regular=20
  expression module in the standard library: <TT class=3Dmodule>re</TT>. =

  <P></P>
  <LI>An interesting feature of string formatting is the ability to use=20
  dictionaries to supply the values that are inserted. Here is an =
example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>names =3D {'tree': 'sycamore', 'flower': =
'poppy', 'herb': 'arugula'}

print 'The tree is %(tree)s' % names
print 'The flower is %(flower)s' % names
print 'The herb is %(herb)s' % names
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004220000000000000000>2.2.2 Sequences</A> </H2>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004221000000000000000>2.2.2.1 What</A> </H3>
<P>There are several types of sequences in Python. We've already =
discussed=20
strings. In this section we will describe lists and tuples. See <A =
class=3Durl=20
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/typesseq.html">http://www.p=
ython.org/doc/current/lib/typesseq.html</A>=20
for a description of the other sequence types (e.g. buffers and xrange =
objects).=20

<P>Lists are dynamic arrays. They are arrays in the sense that you can =
index=20
items in a list (for example "<TT class=3Dsamp>mylist[3]</TT>") and you =
can select=20
sub-ranges (for example "<TT class=3Dsamp>mylist[2:4]</TT>"). They are =
dynamic in=20
the sense that you can add and remove items after the list is created.=20
<P>Tuples are light-weight lists, but differ from lists in that they are =

immutable. That is, once a tuple has been created, you <EM>cannot</EM> =
modify=20
it. You can, of course, modify any (modifiable) objects that the tuple =
refers=20
to.=20
<P>Capabilities of lists:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Append items.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Insert items.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Add a list of items.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>Capabilities of lists and tuples:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Index items.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Select a subsequence of items (also known as a slice).=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Iterate over the items in the list or tuple.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004222000000000000000>2.2.2.2 When</A> </H3>
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Whenever you want to process a colletion of items.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Whenever you want to iterate over a collection of items.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Whenever you want to <EM>index</EM> into a collection of items.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004223000000000000000>2.2.2.3 How</A> </H3>
<P>To create a list use:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; items =3D [111, 222, 333]
&gt;&gt;&gt; items
[111, 222, 333]
</PRE></DIV>
<P>To add an item to the end of a list, use:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; items.append(444)
&gt;&gt;&gt; items
[111, 222, 333, 444]
</PRE></DIV>
<P>To insert an item into a list, use:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; items.insert(0, -1)
&gt;&gt;&gt; items
[-1, 111, 222, 333, 444]
</PRE></DIV>
<P>You can also push items onto the right end of a list and pop items =
off the=20
right end of a list with <TT class=3Dmethod>append</TT> and <TT=20
class=3Dmethod>pop</TT>.=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; items.append(555)
&gt;&gt;&gt; items
[-1, 111, 222, 333, 444, 555]
&gt;&gt;&gt; items.pop()
555
&gt;&gt;&gt; items
[-1, 111, 222, 333, 444]
</PRE></DIV>
<P>And, you can iterate over the items in a list with the =
<CODE>for</CODE>=20
statement:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; for item in items:
...   print 'item:', item
...
item: -1
item: 111
item: 222
item: 333
item: 444
</PRE></DIV>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004230000000000000000>2.2.3 Dictionaries</A> </H2>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004231000000000000000>2.2.3.1 What</A> </H3>
<P>Associative arrays.=20
<P>Capabilities:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Ability to iterate over keys or values.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Ability to add key-value pairs dynamically.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Look-up by key.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>For help on dictionaries, type:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; help dict
</PRE></DIV>
<P>at Python's interactive prompt, or:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>$ pydoc help
</PRE></DIV>
<P>at the command line.=20
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004232000000000000000>2.2.3.2 When</A> </H3>
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>When you need look-up by key.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>When you need a "structured" lite-weight object or an object with =
named=20
  fields. (But, don't forget classes.)=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>When you need to map a name or label to any kind of object, even =
an=20
  executable one such as a function.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004233000000000000000>2.2.3.3 How</A> </H3>
<P>Create a dictionary with:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; lookup =3D {}
&gt;&gt;&gt; lookup
{}
</PRE></DIV>
<P>or:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; def fruitfunc():
...    print "I'm a fruit."
&gt;&gt;&gt; def vegetablefunc():
...    print "I'm a vegetable."
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; lookup =3D {'fruit': fruitfunc, 'vegetable': vegetablefunc}
&gt;&gt;&gt; lookup
{'vegetable': &lt;function vegetablefunc at 0x4028980c&gt;,
'fruit': &lt;function fruitfunc at 0x4028e614&gt;}
&gt;&gt;&gt; lookup['fruit']()
I'm a fruit.
&gt;&gt;&gt; lookup['vegetable']()
I'm a vegetable.
</PRE></DIV>
<P>or:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; lookup =3D dict((('aa', 11), =
('bb', 22), ('cc', 33)))
&gt;&gt;&gt; lookup
{'aa': 11, 'cc': 33, 'bb': 22}
&gt;&gt;&gt;
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Test for the existence of a key with:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; if lookup.has_key('fruit'):
...   print 'contains key "fruit"'
...
contains key "fruit"
&gt;&gt;&gt;
</PRE></DIV>
<P>or:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; if 'fruit' in lookup:
...   print 'contains key "fruit"'
...
contains key "fruit"
&gt;&gt;&gt;
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Access the value of a key as follows:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; print lookup['fruit']
&lt;function fruitfunc at 0x4028e614&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;
</PRE></DIV>
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; for key in lookup:
...     print 'key: %s' % key
...     lookup[key]()
...
key: vegetable
I'm a vegetable.
key: fruit
I'm a fruit.
&gt;&gt;&gt;
</PRE></DIV>
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE></PRE></DIV>
<P>And, remember that you can sub-class dictionaries. Here are two =
versions of=20
the same example. The keyword arguments in the second version require =
Python 2.3=20
or later:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>#
# This example works with Python 2.2.
class MyDict_for_python_22(dict):
    def __init__(self, **kw):
        for key in kw.keys():
            self[key] =3D kw[key]
    def show(self):
        print 'Showing example for Python 2.2 ...'
        for key in self.keys():
            print 'key: %s  value: %s' % (key, self[key])

def test_for_python_22():
    d =3D MyDict_for_python_22(one=3D11, two=3D22, three=3D33)
    d.show()

test_for_python_22()

#
# This example works with Python 2.3.
#   Keyword support, when subclassing dictionaries, seems to have
#   been enhanced in Python 2.3.
class MyDict(dict):
    def show(self):
        print 'Showing example for Python 2.3 ...'
        for key in self.keys():
            print 'key: %s  value: %s' % (key, self[key])

def test():
    d =3D MyDict(one=3D11, two=3D22, three=3D33)
    d.show()

test()
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_dict_example_=
1.txt"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_dict_example_1.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
<P>Running this example produces:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>Showing example for Python 2.2 ...
key: one  value: 11
key: three  value: 33
key: two  value: 22
Showing example for Python 2.3 ...
key: three  value: 33
key: two  value: 22
key: one  value: 11
</PRE></DIV>
<P>A few comments about this example:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>The class <TT class=3Dclass>MyDict</TT> does not define a =
constructor (<TT=20
  class=3Dmethod>__init__</TT>). This enables us to re-use the =
contructor from <TT=20
  class=3Dclass>dict</TT> and any of its forms. Type "<TT =
class=3Dsamp>help=20
  dict</TT>" at the Python interactive prompt to learn about the various =
ways to=20
  call the <TT class=3Dclass>dict</TT> constructor.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>The <TT class=3Dmethod>show</TT> method is the specialization =
added to our=20
  sub-class.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>In our sub-class, we can refer to any methods in the super-class =
(<TT=20
  class=3Dclass>dict</TT>). For example: "<TT =
class=3Dsamp>self.keys()</TT>".=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>In our sub-class, we can refer the dictionary itself. For example: =
"<TT=20
  class=3Dsamp>self[key]</TT>".=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H1><A name=3DSECTION004300000000000000000>2.3 Simple Statements</A> =
</H1>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004310000000000000000>2.3.1 print</A> </H2>
<P>The <CODE>print</CODE> statement sends output to <CODE>stdout</CODE>. =

<P>Here are a few examples:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>print obj
print obj1, obj2, obj3
print "My name is %s" % name
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Notes:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>To print multiple items, separate them with commas. The =
<CODE>print</CODE>=20
  statement inserts a blank between objects.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>The <CODE>print</CODE> statement automatically appends a newline =
to=20
  output. To print without a newline, add a comma after the last object, =
or use=20
  "<TT class=3Dsamp>sys.stdout</TT>", for example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>print 'Output with no newline',
</PRE></DIV>
  <P>which will append a blank, or:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>import sys
sys.stdout.write("Some output")
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>To re-define the destination of output from the <CODE>print</CODE> =

  statement, replace <TT class=3Dmember>sys.stdout</TT> with an instance =
of a=20
  class that supports the <TT class=3Dmethod>write</TT> method. For =
example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>import sys

class Writer:
    def __init__(self, filename):
        self.filename =3D filename
    def write(self, msg):
        f =3D file(self.filename, 'a')
        f.write(msg)
        f.close()

sys.stdout =3D Writer('tmp.log')
print 'Log message #1'
print 'Log message #2'
print 'Log message #3'
</PRE>
  <DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_print_sysstdo=
ut.txt"=20
  type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
  class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_print_sysstdout.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>More information on the <CODE>print</CODE> statement is at <A =
class=3Durl=20
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/ref/print.html">http://www.pyth=
on.org/doc/current/ref/print.html</A>.=20

<P><SPAN class=3Dnote><B class=3Dlabel>Note:</B> Note to Jython users - =
Jython does=20
not appear to support the <TT class=3Dfunction>file</TT> constructor for =
files. In=20
the above example, replace <TT class=3Dfunction>file</TT> with <TT=20
class=3Dfunction>open</TT>.</SPAN>=20
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004320000000000000000>2.3.2 import</A> </H2>
<P>The <CODE>import</CODE> statement makes a module and its contents =
available=20
for use.=20
<P>Here are several forms of the import statement:=20
<P>
<DL>
  <DT><STRONG>import test</STRONG>=20
  <DD>Import module <TT class=3Dmodule>test</TT>. Refer to <VAR>x</VAR> =
in <TT=20
  class=3Dmodule>test</TT> with "<TT class=3Dsamp>test.x</TT>".=20
  <P></P>
  <DT><STRONG>from test import x</STRONG>=20
  <DD>Import <VAR>x</VAR> from <TT class=3Dmodule>test</TT>. Refer to =
<VAR>x</VAR>=20
  in <TT class=3Dmodule>test</TT> with "<TT class=3Dsamp>x</TT>".=20
  <P></P>
  <DT><STRONG>from test import *</STRONG>=20
  <DD>Import all objects from <TT class=3Dmodule>test</TT>. Refer to =
<VAR>x</VAR>=20
  in <TT class=3Dmodule>test</TT> with "<TT class=3Dsamp>x</TT>". This =
form of=20
  import is usually <EM>not</EM> recommended.=20
  <P></P>
  <DT><STRONG>import test as theTest</STRONG>=20
  <DD>Import <TT class=3Dmodule>test</TT>; make it available as <TT=20
  class=3Dmodule>theTest</TT>. Refer to object <VAR>x</VAR> with "<TT=20
  class=3Dsamp>theTest.x</TT>".=20
  <P></P></DD></DL>
<P>A few comments about <CODE>import</CODE>:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>The <CODE>import</CODE> statement also evaluates the code in the =
imported=20
  module.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>But, the code in a module is only evaluated the first time it is =
imported=20
  in a program. So, for example, if a module <SPAN =
class=3Dfile>mymodule.py</SPAN>=20
  is imported from two other modules in a program, the statements in <TT =

  class=3Dmodule>mymodule</TT> will be evaluated only the first time it =
is=20
  imported.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>If you need even <EM>more</EM> variety that the =
<CODE>import</CODE>=20
  statement offers, see the <TT class=3Dmodule>imp</TT> module. =
Documentation at=20
  <A class=3Durl=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/module-imp.html">http://www=
.python.org/doc/current/lib/module-imp.html</A>.=20
  Also see the <CODE>__import__</CODE> built-in function. Documentation =
at <A=20
  class=3Durl=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/built-in-funcs.html">http:/=
/www.python.org/doc/current/lib/built-in-funcs.html</A>.=20

  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>More information on <CODE>import</CODE> at <A class=3Durl=20
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/ref/import.html">http://www.pyt=
hon.org/doc/current/ref/import.html</A>.=20

<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004330000000000000000>2.3.3 assert</A> </H2>
<P>Use the <CODE>assert</CODE> statement to place error checking =
statements in=20
you code. Here is an example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>def test(arg1, arg2):
    arg1 =3D float(arg1)
    arg2 =3D float(arg2)
    assert arg2 !=3D 0, 'Bad dividend -- arg1: %f arg2: %f' % (arg1, =
arg2)
    ratio =3D arg1 / arg2
    print 'ratio:', ratio
</PRE></DIV>
<P>When <VAR>arg2</VAR> is zero, running this code will produce =
something like=20
the following:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "tmp.py", line 22, in ?
    main()
  File "tmp.py", line 18, in main
    test(args[0], args[1])
  File "tmp.py", line 8, in test
    assert arg2 !=3D 0, 'Bad dividend -- arg1: %f arg2: %f' % (arg1, =
arg2)
AssertionError: Bad dividend -- arg1: 2.000000 arg2: 0.000000
</PRE></DIV>
<P>A few comments:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Notice that the trace-back identifies the file and line where the =
test is=20
  made and shows the test itself.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>If you run python with the optimize options (<B =
class=3Dprogramopt>-O</B>=20
  and <B class=3Dprogramopt>-OO</B>), the assertion test is not =
performed.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>The second argument to assert is optional.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004340000000000000000>2.3.4 global</A> </H2>
<P>The problem -- Imagine a global variable <VAR>NAME</VAR>. If, in a =
function,=20
the first mention of that variable is "<TT class=3Dsamp>name =3D =
NAME</TT>", then=20
I'll get the value of the the global variable <VAR>NAME</VAR>. But, if, =
in a=20
function, my first mention of that variable is an assignment <EM>to</EM> =
that=20
variable, then I will create a new <EM>local</EM> variable, and will not =
refer=20
to the global variable at all. Consider:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>NAME =3D "Peach"

def show_global():
    name =3D NAME
    print '(show_global) name: %s' % name

def set_global():
    NAME =3D 'Nectarine'
    name =3D NAME
    print '(set_global) name: %s' % name

show_global()
set_global()
show_global()
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_global_1.txt"=
=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_global_1.py</SPAN>).</A> </DIV></DIV>
<P>Running this code produces:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>(show_global) name: Peach
(set_global) name: Nectarine
(show_global) name: Peach
</PRE></DIV>
<P>The <TT class=3Dfunction>set_global</TT> modifies a local variable =
and=20
<EM>not</EM> the global variable as I might have intended.=20
<P>The solution -- How can I fix that? Here is how:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>NAME =3D "Peach"

def show_global():
    name =3D NAME
    print '(show_global) name: %s' % name

def set_global():
    global NAME
    NAME =3D 'Nectarine'
    name =3D NAME
    print '(set_global) name: %s' % name

show_global()
set_global()
show_global()
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_global_2.txt"=
=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_global_2.py</SPAN>).</A> </DIV></DIV>
<P>Notice the <CODE>global</CODE> statement in function <TT=20
class=3Dfunction>set_global</TT>. Running this code does modify the =
global=20
variable <VAR>NAME</VAR>, and produces the following output:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>(show_global) name: Peach
(set_global) name: Nectarine
(show_global) name: Nectarine
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Comments:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>You can list more than one veriable in the <CODE>global</CODE> =
statement.=20
  For example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>global NAME1, NAME2, NAME3
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H1><A name=3DSECTION004400000000000000000>2.4 Control Structures</A> =
</H1>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004410000000000000000>2.4.1 if</A> </H2>
<P>The <CODE>if</CODE> statement enables us to execute code (or not) =
depending=20
on a condition.=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>"<TT class=3Dsamp>if condition: ...</TT>"=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>"<TT class=3Dsamp>if condition: ... else: ...</TT>"=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>"<TT class=3Dsamp>if condition1: ... elif condition2: ... else: =
...</TT>"=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>Here is an example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; y =3D 25
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; if y &gt; 15:
...     print 'y is large'
... else:
...     print 'y is small'
...
y is large
</PRE></DIV>
<P>A few notes:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>The condition can be any expression, i.e. something that returns a =
value.=20
  A detailed description of expressions can be found at <A class=3Durl=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/ref/expressions.html">http://ww=
w.python.org/doc/current/ref/expressions.html</A>.=20

  <P></P>
  <LI>Parentheses are <EM>not</EM> needed around the condition. Use =
parentheses=20
  to group sub-expressions and control order of evaluation when the =
natural=20
  operator precedence is not what you want. Python's operator=20
  precedences&nbsp;are described at <A class=3Durl=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/ref/summary.html">http://www.py=
thon.org/doc/current/ref/summary.html</A>.=20

  <P></P>
  <LI>Python has no <CODE>switch</CODE> statement. Use =
<CODE>if-elif</CODE>. Or=20
  consider using a dictionary, for example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>def function1():
    print "Hi. I'm function1."
def function2():
    print "Hi. I'm function2."
def function3():
    print "Hi. I'm function3."

mapper =3D {'one': function1, 'two': function2, 'three': function3}

while 1:
    code =3D raw_input('Enter "one", "two", "three", or "quit": ')
    if code =3D=3D 'quit':
        break
    if code not in mapper:
        continue
    mapper[code]()
</PRE>
  <DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_if_switch.txt=
"=20
  type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
  class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_if_switch.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004420000000000000000>2.4.2 for</A> </H2>
<P>The <CODE>for</CODE> statement enables us to iterate over =
collections. It=20
enables us to repeat a set of lines of code once for each item in a =
collection.=20
Collections are things like strings (arrays of characters), lists, =
tuples, and=20
dictionaries.=20
<P>Here is an example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; collection =3D [111,222,333]
&gt;&gt;&gt; for item in collection:
...     print 'item:', item
...
item: 111
item: 222
item: 333
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Comments:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>You can iterate over strings, lists, and tuples.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Iterate over the keys or values in a dictionary with "<TT=20
  class=3Dsamp>aDict.keys()</TT>" and "<TT =
class=3Dsamp>aDict.values()</TT>". Here=20
  is an example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; aDict =3D {'cat': 'furry and =
cute', 'dog': 'friendly and smart'}
&gt;&gt;&gt; aDict.keys()
['dog', 'cat']
&gt;&gt;&gt; aDict.values()
['friendly and smart', 'furry and cute']
&gt;&gt;&gt; for key in aDict.keys():
...     print 'A %s is %s.' % (key, aDict[key])
...
A dog is friendly and smart.
A cat is furry and cute.
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>In recent versions of Python, a dictionary itself is an iterator =
for its=20
  keys. Therefore, you can also do the following:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; for key in aDict:
...     print 'A %s is %s.' % (key, aDict[key])
...
A dog is friendly and smart.
A cat is furry and cute.
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>And, in recent versions of Python, a file is also an iterator over =
the=20
  lines in the file. Therefore, you can do the following:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; infile =3D file('tmp.txt', =
'r')
&gt;&gt;&gt; for line in infile:
...       print line,
...=20
This is line #1
This is line #2
This is line #3
&gt;&gt;&gt; infile.close()
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>There are other kinds of iterations. For example, the built-in <TT =

  class=3Dfunction>iter</TT> will produce an iterator from a collection. =
Here is=20
  an example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; anIter =3D iter([11,22,33])
&gt;&gt;&gt; for item in anIter:
...     print 'item:', item
...
item: 11
item: 22
item: 33
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>You can also implement iterators of your own. To do so, define a =
function=20
  that returns values with <CODE>yield</CODE> (instead of with=20
  <CODE>return</CODE>). Here is an example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; def t(collection):
...     icollection =3D iter(collection)
...     for item in icollection:
...         yield '||%s||' % item
...
&gt;&gt;&gt; for x in t(collection): print x
...
||111||
||222||
||333||
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004430000000000000000>2.4.3 while</A> </H2>
<P><CODE>while</CODE> is another repeating statement. It executes a =
block of=20
code until a condition is false.=20
<P>Here is an example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; reply =3D 'repeat'
&gt;&gt;&gt; while reply =3D=3D 'repeat':
...     print 'Hello'
...     reply =3D raw_input('Enter "repeat" to do it again: ')
...
Hello
Enter "repeat" to do it again: repeat
Hello
Enter "repeat" to do it again: bye
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Comments:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Use the <CODE>break</CODE> statement to exit from a loop. This =
works for=20
  both <CODE>for</CODE> and <CODE>while</CODE>. Here is an example that =
uses=20
  <CODE>break</CODE> in a <CODE>for</CODE> statement:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE># for_break.py
"""Count lines until a line that begins with a double #.
"""

import sys

def countLines(infilename):
    infile =3D file(infilename, 'r')
    count =3D 0
    for line in infile.readlines():
        line =3D line.strip()
        if line[:2] =3D=3D '##':
            break
        count +=3D 1
    return count

def usage():
    print 'Usage: python python_101_for_break.py &lt;infilename&gt;'
    sys.exit(-1)

def main():
    args =3D sys.argv[1:]
    if len(args) !=3D 1:
        usage()
    count =3D countLines(args[0])
    print 'count:', count

if __name__ =3D=3D '__main__':
    main()
</PRE>
  <DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_for_break.txt=
"=20
  type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
  class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_for_break.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>Use the <CODE>continue</CODE> statement to skip the remainder of =
the code=20
  block in a <CODE>for</CODE> or <CODE>while</CODE>. A =
<CODE>continue</CODE> is=20
  a short-circuit which, in effect, branches back to the top of the=20
  <CODE>for</CODE> or <CODE>while</CODE> (or if you prefer, to the end =
of the=20
  block).=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>The test "<TT class=3Dsamp>if __name__ =3D=3D '__main__':</TT>" is =
used to=20
  enable the script to both be (1) imported and (2) run from the command =
line.=20
  That condition is true only when the script is run, but not imported. =
This is=20
  a common Python idiom, which you should consider including at the end =
of your=20
  scripts, whether (1) to give your users a demonstration of what your =
script=20
  does and how to use it or (2) to provide a test of the script.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004440000000000000000>2.4.4 try-except and raise -- =

Exceptions</A> </H2>
<P>Use a <CODE>try:except:</CODE> block to catch an exception.=20
<P>Use <CODE>raise</CODE> to raise an exception.=20
<P>Comments and hints:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Catch all exceptions with a "bare" <CODE>except:</CODE>. For =
example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; try:
...     x =3D y
... except:
...     print 'y not defined'
...
y not defined
</PRE></DIV>
  <P>Note, however, that it is usually better to catch specific =
exceptions.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Catch a specific error by refering to an exception class in the=20
  <CODE>except:</CODE>. To determine what error or exception you want to =
catch,=20
  generate it and try it. Because Python reports errors with a walk-back =
that=20
  ends with reporting the exception, you can learn which exception to =
catch. For=20
  example, suppose I want to learn which exception is thrown when a =
Python can't=20
  open a file. I can try the following from the interactive prompt:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; myfile =3D =
file('amissingfile.py', 'r')
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "&lt;stdin&gt;", line 1, in ?
IOError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'amissingfile.py'
</PRE></DIV>
  <P>So, now I know that I can do:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; try:
...     myfile =3D file('amissingfile.py', 'r')
... except IOError:
...     print 'amissingfile.py is missing'
...
amissingfile.py is missing
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>Catch any one of several execption type. Example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>try:
    #f =3D open('abcdexyz.txt', 'r')
    d =3D {}
    x =3D d['name']
except (IOError, KeyError), e:
    print 'The error is --', e
</PRE></DIV>
  <P>Note that multiple exceptions to be caught by a single =
<CODE>except:</CODE>=20
  clause are in parentheses; they are a tuple.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>You can customize your error handling still further by passing an =
object=20
  on the <CODE>raise</CODE> and catching that object in the=20
  <CODE>except:</CODE>. By doing so, you can pass information up from =
the=20
  <CODE>raise</CODE> statement to an exception handler. One way of doing =
this is=20
  to pass an object. A reasonable strategy is to define a sub-class of a =

  standard exception. For example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; class E(RuntimeError):
...   def __init__(self, msg):
...     self.msg =3D msg
...   def getMsg(self):
...     return self.msg
...
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; try:
...   raise E('my test error')
... except E, obj:
...   print 'Msg:', obj.getMsg()
...
Msg: my test error
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>If you catch an exception using <CODE>try:except:</CODE>, but then =
find=20
  that you do <EM>not</EM> want to handle the exception at that =
location, you=20
  can "re-raise" the <EM>same</EM> exception (with the same arguments) =
by using=20
  <CODE>raise</CODE> with no arguments. An example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>class GeneralException(Exception):
    pass
class SimpleException(GeneralException):
    pass
class ComplexException(GeneralException):
    pass

def some_func_that_throws_exceptions():
    #raise SimpleException, 'this is a simple error'
    raise ComplexException, 'this is a complex error'

def test():
    try:
        some_func_that_throws_exceptions()
    except GeneralException, e:
        if isinstance(e, SimpleException):
            print e
        else:
            raise

test()
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004450000000000000000>2.4.5 Reading Text Files</A> =
</H2>
<P>To read a text file, first create a file object. Here is an example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>inFile =3D file('messages.log', 'r')
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Then use one or more of the file object's methods to process the =
contents of=20
the file. Here are a few strategies:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Use "<TT class=3Dsamp>inFile.read()</TT>" to get the entire =
contents of the=20
  file (a string). Example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; inFile =3D file('tmp.txt', =
'r')
&gt;&gt;&gt; content =3D inFile.read()
&gt;&gt;&gt; inFile.close()
&gt;&gt;&gt; print content
aaa bbb ccc
ddd eee fff
ggg hhh iii

&gt;&gt;&gt; words =3D content.split()
&gt;&gt;&gt; print words
['aaa', 'bbb', 'ccc', 'ddd', 'eee', 'fff', 'ggg', 'hhh', 'iii']
&gt;&gt;&gt; for word in words:
...     print word
...
aaa
bbb
ccc
ddd
eee
fff
ggg
hhh
iii
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>Use "<TT class=3Dsamp>for line in inFile:</TT>" to process one =
line at a=20
  time. You can do this because (at least since Python 2.3) file objects =
obey=20
  the iterator protocol, that is they support methods <TT=20
  class=3Dmethod>__iter__</TT> and <TT class=3Dmethod>next</TT>. For =
more on the=20
  iterator protocol see <A class=3Durl=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/typeiter.html">http://www.p=
ython.org/doc/current/lib/typeiter.html</A>.=20

  <P>Example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; inFile =3D file('tmp.txt', =
'r')
&gt;&gt;&gt; for line in inFile:
...     print 'Line:', line,
...
Line: aaaaa
Line: bbbbb
Line: ccccc
Line: ddddd
Line: eeeee
&gt;&gt;&gt; inFile.close()
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>For earlier versions of Python, use "<TT=20
  class=3Dsamp>inFile.readlines()</TT>" or "<TT=20
  class=3Dsamp>inFile.xreadlines()</TT>".=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>If your want to get the contents of an entire text file as a =
collection of=20
  lines, use <TT class=3Dfunction>readlines</TT>. Example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; inFile =3D file('tmp.txt', =
'r')
&gt;&gt;&gt; lines =3D inFile.readlines()
&gt;&gt;&gt; print lines
['aaaaa\n', 'bbbbb\n', 'ccccc\n', 'ddddd\n', 'eeeee\n']
&gt;&gt;&gt; print lines[2]
ccccc

&gt;&gt;&gt; inFile.close()
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004460000000000000000>2.4.6 Iterator objects</A> =
</H2>
<P><SPAN class=3Dnote><B class=3Dlabel>Note:</B> You will need a =
sufficiently recent=20
version of Python in order to use iterators and generators. I believe =
that they=20
were introduced in Python 2.2.</SPAN>=20
<P>Goals for this section:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Learn how to implement a generator function, that is, a function =
which,=20
  when called, returns an iterator.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Learn how to implement a class containing a generator method, that =
is, a=20
  method which, when called, returns an iterator.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Learn the iterator protocol, specifically what methods an iterator =
must=20
  support and what those methods must do.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Learn how to implement an iterator class, that is, a class whose =
instances=20
  are iterator objects.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Learn how to implement recursive iterator generators, that is, an =
iterator=20
  generator which recursively produces iterator generators.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Learn that your implementation of an iterator object (an iterator =
class)=20
  can "refresh" itself and learn at least one way to do this.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>Definitions:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI><EM>Iterator</EM> - And iterator is an object that satisfies =
(implements)=20
  the iterator protocol.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI><EM>Iterator protocol</EM> - An object implements the iterator =
protocol if=20
  it implements both a <CODE>next</CODE> and a <CODE>__iter__</CODE> =
method=20
  which satisfy these rules: (1) the <CODE>__iter__</CODE> method must =
return=20
  the iterator; (2) the <CODE>next</CODE> method should return each of =
the items=20
  to be iterated over and when finished should raise the=20
  <CODE>StopIteration</CODE> exception. The iterator protocol is =
described in <A=20
  class=3Dulink =
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/typeiter.html">2.3.5=20
  Iterator Types</A>.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI><EM>Iterator class</EM> - A class that implements (satisfies) the =
iterator=20
  protocol. In particular, the class implements <CODE>next</CODE> and=20
  <CODE>__iter__</CODE> methods as described above and in <A =
class=3Dulink=20
  href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/typeiter.html">2.3.5 =
Iterator=20
  Types</A>.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI><EM>(Iterator) generator function</EM> - A function (or method) =
which,=20
  when called, returns an iterator object, that is, an object that =
satisfies the=20
  iterator protocol. A function containing a <CODE>yield</CODE> =
statement=20
  automatically becomes a generator.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI><EM>Generator expression</EM> - An expression which produces an =
iterator=20
  object. Generator expressions have a form similar to an list =
comprehension,=20
  but are enclosed in parentheses rather than square brackets. See =
example=20
  below.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>A few additional basic points:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>A function that contains a <CODE>yield</CODE> statement is a =
generator=20
  function. When called, it returns an iterator, that is, an object that =

  provides <CODE>next</CODE> and <CODE>__iter__</CODE> methods.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>A class that defines both a <CODE>next</CODE> method and a=20
  <CODE>__iter__</CODE> method satisfies the iterator protocol. So, =
instances of=20
  such a class will be iterators.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Python provides a variety of ways to produce iterators. This =
section=20
  describes a few of those ways. You should also look at the <TT=20
  class=3Dfunction>iter</TT> built-in function, which is described in <A =

  class=3Dulink =
href=3D"http://docs.python.org/lib/built-in-funcs.html">2.1 Built-in=20
  Functions</A>.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>An iterator can be used in an iterator context, for example in a=20
  <CODE>for</CODE> statement, in a list comprehension, and in a =
generator=20
  expression. When an iterator is used in an iterator context, the =
iterator=20
  produces its values.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>This section attempts to provide examples that illustrate the=20
generator/iterator pattern.=20
<P>Why is this important?=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Once mastered, it is a simple, convenient, and powerful =
programming=20
  pattern.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>It has many and pervasive uses.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>It helps to lexically separate the producer code from the consumer =
code.=20
  Doing so makes it easier to locate problems and to modify or fix code =
in a way=20
  that is localized and does not have unwanted side-effects.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Implementing your own iterators (and generators) enables you to =
define=20
  your own <EM>abstract</EM> sequences, that is, sequences whose =
composition are=20
  defined by your computations rather than by their presence in a =
container. In=20
  fact, your iterator can calculate or retrieve values as each one is =
requested.=20

  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>Examples - The remainder of this section provides a set of examples.=20
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004461000000000000000>2.4.6.1 Example - A generator =

function</A> </H3>
<P>This function contains a <CODE>yield</CODE> statement. Therefore, =
when we=20
call it, it produces an iterator.=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>def generateItems(seq):
    for item in seq:
        yield 'item: %s' % item

anIter =3D generateItems([])
print 'dir(anIter):', dir(anIter)
anIter =3D generateItems([111,222,333])
for x in anIter:
    print x
anIter =3D generateItems(['aaa', 'bbb', 'ccc'])
print anIter.next()
print anIter.next()
print anIter.next()
print anIter.next()
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_iterator_gene=
rator.txt"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_iterator_generator.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
<P>Running this example produces the following output:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>dir(anIter): ['__class__', '__delattr__', =
'__doc__', '__getattribute__',
'__hash__', '__init__', '__iter__', '__new__', '__reduce__',
'__reduce_ex__', '__repr__', '__setattr__', '__str__', 'gi_frame',
'gi_running', 'next']
item: 111
item: 222
item: 333
item: aaa
item: bbb
item: ccc
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "iterator_generator.py", line 14, in ?
    print anIter.next()
StopIteration
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Notes and explanation:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>The value returned by the call to the generator (function) is an =
iterator.=20
  It obeys the iterator protocol. That is, "<TT =
class=3Dsamp>dir(anIter)</TT>"=20
  shows that it has both <TT class=3Dmethod>__iter__</TT> and <TT=20
  class=3Dmethod>next</TT> methods.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Because this object is an iterator, we can use a <CODE>for</CODE>=20
  statement to iterate over the values returned by the generator.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>We can also get its values by repeatedly calling the <TT=20
  class=3Dmethod>next</TT> method, until it raises the =
<CODE>StopIteration</CODE>=20
  exception. This ability to call the <TT class=3Dmethod>next</TT> =
method enables=20
  us to pass the iterator object around and get values at different =
locations in=20
  our code.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Once we have obtained all the values from an iterator, it is, in =
effect,=20
  "empty" or "exhausted". The iterator protocol, in fact, specifies that =
once an=20
  iterator raises the <CODE>StopIteration</CODE> exception, it should =
continue=20
  to do so. Another way to say this is that there is no "rewind" =
operation. But,=20
  you can call the the generator function again to get a "fresh" =
iterator.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004462000000000000000>2.4.6.2 Example - A class =
containing a=20
generator method</A> </H3>
<P>Each time this method is called, it produces a (new) iterator object. =
This=20
method is analogous to the <TT class=3Dmethod>iterkeys</TT> and <TT=20
class=3Dmethod>itervalues</TT> methods in the dictionary built-in =
object.=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>#
# A class that provides an iterator generator method.
#
class Node:
    def __init__(self, name=3D'&lt;noname&gt;', =
value=3D'&lt;novalue&gt;', children=3DNone):
        self.name =3D name
        self.value =3D value
        self.children =3D children
        if children is None:
            self.children =3D []
        else:
            self.children =3D children
    def set_name(self, name): self.name =3D name
    def get_name(self): return self.name
    def set_value(self, value): self.value =3D value
    def get_value(self): return self.value
    def iterchildren(self):
        for child in self.children:
            yield child
    #
    # Print information on this node and walk over all children and
    #   grandchildren ...
    def walk(self, level=3D0):
        print '%sname: %s  value: %s' % (
            get_filler(level), self.get_name(), self.get_value(), )
        for child in self.iterchildren():
            child.walk(level + 1)

#
# An function that is the equivalent of the walk() method in
#   class Node.
#
def walk(node, level=3D0):
    print '%sname: %s  value: %s' % (
        get_filler(level), node.get_name(), node.get_value(), )
    for child in node.iterchildren():
        walk(child, level + 1)

def get_filler(level):
    return '    ' * level

def test():
    a7 =3D Node('gilbert', '777')
    a6 =3D Node('fred', '666')
    a5 =3D Node('ellie', '555')
    a4 =3D Node('daniel', '444')
    a3 =3D Node('carl', '333', [a4, a5])
    a2 =3D Node('bill', '222', [a6, a7])
    a1 =3D Node('alice', '111', [a2, a3])
    # Use the walk method to walk the entire tree.
    print 'Using the method:'
    a1.walk()
    print '=3D' * 30
    # Use the walk function to walk the entire tree.
    print 'Using the function:'
    walk(a1)

test()

</PRE>
