<P>Notes and explanation:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>This class contains a method <TT class=3Dmethod>iterchildren</TT> =
which,=20
  when called, returns an iterator.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>The <CODE>yield</CODE> statement in the method <TT=20
  class=3Dmethod>iterchildren</TT> makes it into a generator.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>The <CODE>yield</CODE> statement returns one item each time it is =
reached.=20
  The next time the iterator object is "called" it resumes immediately =
after the=20
  <CODE>yield</CODE> statement.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>A function may have any number of <CODE>yield</CODE> statements.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>A <CODE>for</CODE> statement will iterate over all the items =
produced by=20
  an iterator object.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>This example shows two ways to use the generator, specifically: =
(1) the=20
  <TT class=3Dmethod>walk</TT> method in the class <TT =
class=3Dclass>Node</TT> and=20
  (2) the <TT class=3Dfunction>walk</TT> function. Both call the =
generator <TT=20
  class=3Dmethod>iterchildren</TT> and both do pretty much the same =
thing.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004463000000000000000>2.4.6.3 Example - An iterator =
class</A>=20
</H3>
<P>This class implements the iterator protocol. Therefore, instances of =
this=20
class are iterators. The presence of the <TT class=3Dmethod>next</TT> =
and <TT=20
class=3Dmethod>__iter__</TT> methods means that this class implements =
the iterator=20
protocol and makes instances of this class iterators.=20
<P>Note that when an iterator is "exhausted" it, normally, cannot be =
reused to=20
iterate over the sequence. However, in this example, we provide a <TT=20
class=3Dmethod>refresh</TT> method which enables us to "rewind" and =
reuse the=20
iterator instance.=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>#
# An iterator class that does *not* use ``yield``.
#   This iterator produces every other item in a sequence.
#
class IteratorExample:
    def __init__(self, seq):
        self.seq =3D seq
        self.idx =3D 0
    def next(self):
        self.idx +=3D 1
        if self.idx &gt;=3D len(self.seq):
            raise StopIteration
        value =3D self.seq[self.idx]
        self.idx +=3D 1
        return value
    def __iter__(self):
        return self
    def refresh(self):
        self.idx =3D 0

def test_iteratorexample():
    a =3D IteratorExample('edcba')
    for x in a:
        print x
    print '----------'
    a.refresh()
    for x in a:
        print x
    print '=3D' * 30
    a =3D IteratorExample('abcde')
    try:
        print a.next()
        print a.next()
        print a.next()
        print a.next()
        print a.next()
        print a.next()
    except StopIteration, e:
        print 'stopping', e
</PRE>
