<P>Notes and explanation:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>The <TT class=3Dmethod>next</TT> method must keep track of where =
it is and=20
  what item it should produce next.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004464000000000000000>2.4.6.4 Example - An iterator =
class=20
that uses yield</A> </H3>
<P>There may be times when the <TT class=3Dmethod>next</TT> method is =
easier and=20
more straight-forward to implement using yield. If so, then this class =
might=20
serve as an model. If you do not feel the need to do this, then you =
should=20
ignore this example.=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>#
# An iterator class that uses ``yield``.
#   This iterator produces every other item in a sequence.
#
class YieldIteratorExample:
    def __init__(self, seq):
        self.seq =3D seq
        self.iterator =3D self._next()
        self.next =3D self.iterator.next
    def _next(self):
        flag =3D 0
        for x in self.seq:
            if flag:
                flag =3D 0
                yield x
            else:
                flag =3D 1
    def __iter__(self):
        return self.iterator
    def refresh(self):
        self.iterator =3D self._next()
        self.next =3D self.iterator.next

def test_yielditeratorexample():
    a =3D YieldIteratorExample('edcba')
    for x in a:
        print x
    print '----------'
    a.refresh()
    for x in a:
        print x
    print '=3D' * 30
    a =3D YieldIteratorExample('abcde')
    try:
        print a.next()
        print a.next()
        print a.next()
        print a.next()
        print a.next()
        print a.next()
    except StopIteration, e:
        print 'stopping', e

test_yielditeratorexample()
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_yielditerclas=
s.txt"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_yielditerclass.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
<P>Running this example produces the following output:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>d
b
----------
d
b
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D
b
d
stopping
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Notes and explanation:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Because the <TT class=3Dmethod>_next</TT> method uses =
<CODE>yield</CODE>,=20
  calling it (actually, calling the iterator object it produces) in an =
iterator=20
  context causes it to be "resumed" immediately after the =
<CODE>yield</CODE>=20
  statement. This reduces bookkeeping a bit.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>However, with this style, we must explicitly produce an iterator. =
We do=20
  this by calling the <TT class=3Dmethod>_next</TT> method, which =
contains a=20
  <CODE>yield</CODE> statement, and is therefore a generator. The =
following code=20
  in our constructor (<TT class=3Dmethod>__init__</TT>) completes the =
set-up of=20
  our class as an iterator class:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>self.iterator =3D self._next()
self.next =3D self.iterator.next
</PRE></DIV>
  <P>Remember that we need both <TT class=3Dmethod>__iter__</TT> and <TT =

  class=3Dmethod>next</TT> methods in order to satisfy the iterator =
protocol. The=20
  <TT class=3Dmethod>__iter__</TT> is already there and the above code =
in the=20
  constructor creates the <TT class=3Dmethod>next</TT> method.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004465000000000000000>2.4.6.5 Example - A list=20
comprehension</A> </H3>
<P>A list comprehension looks a bit like an iterator, but it produces a =
list.=20
<P>See <A class=3Dulink =
href=3D"http://docs.python.org/ref/lists.html">5.2.4 List=20
displays</A> for more on list comprehensions.=20
<P>Here is an example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>In [4]: def f(x):
   ...:     return x * 3
   ...:
In [5]: list1 =3D [11, 22, 33]
In [6]: list2 =3D [f(x) for x in list1]
In [7]: print list2
[33, 66, 99]
</PRE></DIV>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004466000000000000000>2.4.6.6 Example - A generator =

expression</A> </H3>
<P>A generator expression looks quite similar to a list comprehension, =
but is=20
enclosed in parentheses rather than square brackets. Unlike a list=20
comprehension, a generator expression does <EM>not</EM> produce a list; =
it=20
produces an iterator object.=20
<P>For more on generator expressions, see <A class=3Dulink=20
href=3D"http://docs.python.org/ref/genexpr.html">5.2.5 Generator =
expressions</A>.=20
<P>The following example uses a generator expression to produce an =
iterator.=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>mylist =3D range(10)

def f(x):
    return x*3

genexpr =3D (f(x) for x in mylist)

for x in genexpr:
    print x
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Notes and explanation:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>The generator expression <CODE>(f(x) for x in mylist)</CODE> =
produces an=20
  iterator object.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Notice that we can use the iterator object later in our code, can =
save it=20
  in a data structure, and can pass it to a function.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H1><A name=3DSECTION004500000000000000000>2.5 Organization</A> </H1>
<P>This section describes Python features that you can use to organize =
and=20
structure your code.=20
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004510000000000000000>2.5.1 Functions</A> </H2>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004511000000000000000>2.5.1.1 A basic function</A> =
</H3>
<P>Use <CODE>def</CODE> to define a function. Here is a simple example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>def test(msg, count):
    for idx in range(count):
        print '%s %d' % (msg, idx)

test('Test #', 4)
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Comments:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>After evaluation <CODE>def</CODE> creates a function object.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Call the function using the parentheses function call notation, in =
this=20
  case "<TT class=3Dsamp>test('Test #', 4)</TT>".=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>As with other Python objects, you can stuff a function object into =
other=20
  structures such as tuples, lists, and dictionaries. Here is an =
example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE># Create a tuple:
val =3D (test, 'A label:', 5)

# Call the function:
val[0](val[1], val[2])
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004512000000000000000>2.5.1.2 A function with =
default=20
arguments</A> </H3>
<P>Providing default arguments allows the caller to omit some arguments. =
Here is=20
an example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>def testDefaultArgs(arg1=3D'default1', =
arg2=3D'default2'):
    print 'arg1:', arg1
    print 'arg2:', arg2

testDefaultArgs('Explicit value')
</PRE></DIV>
<P>The above example prints:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>arg1: Explicit value
arg2: default2
</PRE></DIV>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004513000000000000000>2.5.1.3 Argument lists and =
keyword=20
argument lists</A> </H3>
<P>Here is an example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>def testArgLists_1(*args, **kwargs):
    print 'args:', args
    print 'kwargs:', kwargs
           =20
testArgLists_1('aaa', 'bbb', arg1=3D'ccc', arg2=3D'ddd')

def testArgLists_2(arg0, *args, **kwargs):
    print 'arg0: "%s"' % arg0
    print 'args:', args
    print 'kwargs:', kwargs
           =20
print '=3D' * 40
testArgLists_2('a first argument', 'aaa', 'bbb', arg1=3D'ccc', =
arg2=3D'ddd')
</PRE>
