ist.txt"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_function_arglist.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
<P>Running this example displays:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>args: ('aaa', 'bbb')
kwargs: {'arg1': 'ccc', 'arg2': 'ddd'}
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D
arg0: "a first argument"
args: ('aaa', 'bbb')
kwargs: {'arg1': 'ccc', 'arg2': 'ddd'}
</PRE></DIV>
<P>A little guidance:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Positional arguments must proceed all keyword arguments when you =
call the=20
  function.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>You can also have "normal" arguments in the function definition. =
For=20
  example: "<TT class=3Dsamp>def test(arg0, *args, **kwargs):</TT>". See =
the=20
  second example above.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>The keyword argument parameter is a dictionary, so you can do =
anything=20
  with it that you do with a normal dictionary.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004520000000000000000>2.5.2 Classes and =
Instances</A> </H2>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004521000000000000000>2.5.2.1 A basic class</A> =
</H3>
<P>Define a basic class as follows:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>class Basic:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name =3D name
    def show(self):
        print 'Basic -- name: %s' % self.name

obj1 =3D Basic('Apricot')
obj1.show()
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_class_basic_1=
.txt"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_class_basic_1.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
<P>Running the above example produces the following:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>Basic -- name: Apricot
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Explanation:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Methods are added to the class with <CODE>def</CODE>. The first =
argument=20
  to a method is the class instance. By convention it is spelled "self". =

  <P></P>
  <LI>The constructor for a class is a method named "__init__".=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>The <VAR>self</VAR> variable must be explicitly listed as the =
first=20
  argument to a method. You could spell it differently from "self", but =
don't do=20
  so.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Instance variables are referred to with "self.XXX". Notice how in =
our=20
  example an argument to the constructor is saved as an instance =
variable.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>An instance is created by <EM>calling</EM> the class. For example: =
"<TT=20
  class=3Dsamp>obj =3D Basic('Apricot')</TT>".=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>In addition to <CODE>__init__</CODE> there are other special =
method names=20
  of the form "__XXX__", which are used to customize classes and their=20
  instances. These are described at <A class=3Durl=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/ref/specialnames.html">http://w=
ww.python.org/doc/current/ref/specialnames.html</A>.=20

  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>A few more notes on <VAR>self</VAR>:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI><VAR>self</VAR> is a reference to the instance. Think of it (in =
part) as a=20
  reference to the container for the data or state for the object.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>In many object-oriented programming languages, the instance is =
hidden in=20
  the method definitions. These languages typically explain this by =
saying=20
  something like "The instance is passed as an implicit first argument =
to the=20
  method."=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>In Python, the instance is visible and <EM>explicit</EM> in method =

  definitions. You must explicitly declare the instance as the first =
parameter=20
  of each (instance) method. This first parameter is (almost) always =
spelled=20
  "self".=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004522000000000000000>2.5.2.2 Inheritance</A> </H3>
<P>Define a class <CODE>Special</CODE> that inherits from a super-class=20
<CODE>Basic</CODE> as follows:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>class Basic:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name =3D name
    def show(self):
        print 'Basic -- name: %s' % self.name

class Special(Basic):
    def __init__(self, name, edible):
        Basic.__init__(self, name)
        self.upper =3D name.upper()
        self.edible =3D edible
    def show(self):
        Basic.show(self)
        print 'Special -- upper name: %s.' % self.upper,
        if self.edible:
            print "It's edible."
        else:
            print "It's not edible."
    def edible(self):
        return self.edible

obj1 =3D Basic('Apricot')
obj1.show()
print '=3D' * 30
obj2 =3D Special('Peach', 1)
obj2.show()
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_class_basic_2=
.txt"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_class_basic_2.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
<P>Running this example produces the following:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>Basic -- name: Apricot
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D
Basic -- name: Peach
Special -- upper name: PEACH. It's edible.
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Comments:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>The super-class is named after the class name in parentheses. For =
multiple=20
  inheritence, separate the super-classes with commas.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Call a method in the super-class, by-passing the method with the =
same name=20
  in the sub-class, from the sub-class by using the super-class name. =
For=20
  example: "<TT class=3Dsamp>Basic.__init__(self, name)</TT>"and "<TT=20
  class=3Dsamp>Basic.show(self)</TT>".=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>In our example (above), the sub-class (<TT =
class=3Dclass>Special</TT>)=20
  specializes the super-class (<TT class=3Dclass>Basic</TT>) by adding =
additional=20
  member variables (<VAR>self.upper</VAR> and <VAR>self.edible</VAR>) =
and by=20
  adding an additional method (<TT class=3Dmethod>edible</TT>).=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004523000000000000000>2.5.2.3 Class data</A> </H3>
<P>A class data member is a member that has only one value for the class =
and all=20
its instances. Here is an example from the Python FAQ at <A class=3Durl=20
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/FAQ.html">http://www.python.org/doc/FAQ=
.html</A>:=20

<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>class C:
    count =3D 0   # number of times C.__init__ called
    def __init__(self):
        C.count =3D C.count + 1
    def getcount(self):
        return C.count  # or return self.count

c1 =3D C()
print 'Current count:', c1.getcount()
c2 =3D C()
print  'Current count:', c2.getcount()
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_class_advance=
d_1.txt"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_class_advanced_1.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
<P>Running this example produces:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>Current count: 1
Current count: 2
</PRE></DIV>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004524000000000000000></A><A=20
name=3DStatic_methods_and_class_methods></A><BR>2.5.2.4 Static methods =
and class=20
methods </H3>
<P>New-style classes can have static methods and class methods.=20
<P>A new-style class is a class that inherits directly or indirectly =
from <TT=20
class=3Dclass>object</TT> or from a built-in type.=20
<P>Here is an example that shows how to define static methods and class =
methods:=20

<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>class Advanced(object):
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name =3D name
    def Description():
        return 'This is an advanced class.'
    def ClassDescription(cls):
        return 'This is advanced class: %s' % repr(cls)
    Description =3D staticmethod(Description)
    ClassDescription =3D classmethod(ClassDescription)

obj1 =3D Advanced('Nectarine')
print obj1.Description()
print obj1.ClassDescription()
print '=3D' * 30
print Advanced.Description()
print  Advanced.ClassDescription()
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_class_advance=
d_2.txt"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_class_advanced_2.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
<P>Running the above produces the following output:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>This is an advanced class.
This is advanced class: &lt;class __main__.Advanced at 0x401c926c&gt;
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D
This is an advanced class.
This is advanced class: &lt;class __main__.Advanced at 0x401c926c&gt;
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Notes:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>The class inherits from class <TT class=3Dclass>object</TT>, which =
makes it=20
  a new-style class.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Create a static method with "<TT class=3Dsamp>x =3D =
staticmethod(y)</TT>",=20
  where <VAR>y</VAR> is a normal method but without the =
<VAR>self</VAR>/first=20
  parameter.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Create a class method with "<TT class=3Dsamp>x =3D =
classmethod(y)</TT>", where=20
  <VAR>y</VAR> is a normal method.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>The difference between static and class methods is that a class =
method=20
  receives the class (not the instance) as its first argument. A =
summary:=20
  <P>
  <UL>
    <LI>A normal/standard method always receives an instance as its =
first=20
    argument.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI>A class method always receives the class as its first argument.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI>A static method does not (autoatically) receive either the =
instance or=20
    the class as the first argument.=20
    <P></P></LI></UL>
  <P></P>
  <LI>You can call static and class methods using either an instance or =
a class.=20
  In our example either "<TT class=3Dsamp>obj1.Description()</TT>" or =
"<TT=20
  class=3Dsamp>Advanced.Description()</TT>" will work.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>You should also review the relevant standard Python documentation =
which you=20
can find at <A class=3Dulink=20
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/built-in-funcs.html">Python=
 Library=20
Reference - 2.1 Built-in Functions</A>.=20
<P>By now, you are likely to be asking: "Why and when should I use class =
methods=20
and static methods?" Here is a bit of guidance, though=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Most of the time, almost always, implement plain <EM>instance</EM> =

  methods. Implement an instance method whenever the method needs access =
to the=20
  values that are specific to the instance or needs to call other =
methods that=20
  have access to instance specific values. If the method needs =
<VAR>self</VAR>,=20
  then you probably need an instance method.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Implement a <EM>class</EM> method (1) when the method does =
<EM>not</EM>=20
  need access to instance variables and (2) when you do not want to =
require the=20
  caller of the method to create an instance and (3) when the method =
needs=20
  access to class variables. A class method may be called on either an =
instance=20
  or the class. A class method gets the class as a first argument, =
whether it is=20
  called on the class or the instance. If the method needs access to the =
class=20
  but does not need self, then think class method.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Implement a <EM>static</EM> method if you merely want to put the =
code of=20
  the method within the scope of the class, perhaps for purposes of =
organizing=20
  your code, but the method needs access to neither class nor instance =
variables=20
  (though you can access class variables through the class itself). A =
static=20
  method may be called on either an instance or the class. A static =
method gets=20
  neither the class nor the instance as an argument.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>To summarize:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>Implement an instance method, unless ...=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>... the method needs access to class variables but not instance =
variables,=20
  then implement a class method, unless ...=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>... the method needs access to neither instance variables nor =
class=20
  variables and you still want to include it within the class =
definition, then=20
  implement a static method.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Above all, write clear, plain code that will be understandable to =
your=20
  readers. Do <EM>not</EM> use a more confusing language feature and do=20
  <EM>not</EM> force your readers to learn a new language feature unles =
you have=20
  a good reason.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H3><A name=3DSECTION004525000000000000000>2.5.2.5 Properties</A> </H3>
<P>A new-style class can have <EM>properties</EM>. A property is an =
attribute of=20
a class that is associated with a getter and a setter function.=20
<P>Declare the property and its getter and setter functions with <TT=20
class=3Dfunction>property()</TT>.=20
<P>Here is an example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>class A(object):
    count =3D 0
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name =3D name
    def set_name(self, name):
        print 'setting name: %s' % name
        self.name =3D name
    def get_name(self):
        print 'getting name: %s' % self.name
        return self.name
    objname =3D property(get_name, set_name)

def test():
    a =3D A('apple')
    print 'name: %s' % a.objname
    a.objname =3D 'banana'
    print 'name: %s' % a.objname

test()

</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_properties.tx=
t"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_properties.py</SPAN>).</A> </DIV></DIV>
<P>Running the above produces the following output:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>getting name: apple
name: apple
setting name: banana
getting name: banana
name: banana
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Notes:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>The class inherits from class <TT class=3Dclass>object</TT>, which =
makes it=20
  a new-style class.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>When a value is assigned to a property, the setter method is =
called.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>When the value of a property is accessed, the getter method is =
called.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>You can also define a delete method and a documentation attribute =
for a=20
  property. For more information, visit <A class=3Dulink=20
  href=3D"http://docs.python.org/lib/built-in-funcs.html">2.1 Built-in=20
  Functions</A> and look for <TT class=3Dfunction>property</TT>.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004530000000000000000>2.5.3 Modules</A> </H2>
<P>You can use a module to organize a number of Python definitions in a =
single=20
file. Here is an example:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE># python_101_module_simple.py

"""
This simple module contains definitions of a class and several=20
functions.
"""

LABEL =3D '=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D Testing a simple module =3D=3D=3D=3D=3D'

class Person:
    """Sample of a simple class definition.
    """
    def __init__(self, name, description):
        self.name =3D name
        self.description =3D description
    def show(self):
        print 'Person -- name: %s  description: %s' % (self.name, =
self.description)

def test(msg, count):
    """A sample of a simple function.
    """
    for idx in range(count):
        print '%s %d' % (msg, idx)

def testDefaultArgs(arg1=3D'default1', arg2=3D'default2'):
    """A function with default arguments.
    """
    print 'arg1:', arg1
    print 'arg2:', arg2

def testArgLists(*args, **kwargs):
    """
    A function which references the argument list and keyword arguments.
    """
    print 'args:', args
    print 'kwargs:', kwargs

def main():
    """
    A test harness for this module.
    """
    print LABEL
    person =3D Person('Herman', 'A cute guy')
    person.show()
    print '=3D' * 30
    test('Test #', 4)
    print '=3D' * 30
    testDefaultArgs('Explicit value')
    print '=3D' * 30
    testArgLists('aaa', 'bbb', arg1=3D'ccc', arg2=3D'ddd')

if __name__ =3D=3D '__main__':
    main()
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_module_simple=
.txt"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/python_101_module_simple.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
<P>Running the above produces the following output:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D Testing a simple module =
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D
Person -- name: Herman  description: A cute guy
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D
Test # 0
Test # 1
Test # 2
Test # 3
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D
arg1: Explicit value
arg2: default2
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D
args: ('aaa', 'bbb')
kwargs: {'arg1': 'ccc', 'arg2': 'ddd'}
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Comments:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>The string definitions at the beginning of each of the module, =
class=20
  definitions, and function definitions serve as documentation for these =
items.=20
  You can show this documentation with the following from the =
command-line:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>$ pydoc python_101_module_simple
</PRE></DIV>
  <P>Or this, from the Python interactive prompt:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; import =
python_101_module_simple
&gt;&gt;&gt; help(python_101_module_simple)
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P>
  <LI>It is common and it is a good practice to include a test harness =
for the=20
  module at the end of the source file. Note that the test "<TT =
class=3Dsamp>if=20
  __name__ =3D=3D '__main__':</TT>" will be true only when the file is =
run (e.g.=20
  from the command-line with "<TT class=3Dsamp>$ python=20
  python_101_module_simple.py</TT>"), but <EM>not</EM> when the module =
is=20
  imported.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Remember that the code in module is only evaluated the first time =
it is=20
  imported in a program. So, for example, global variables in a module =
cause=20
  behavior that users of the module might not expect.=20
  <P></P>
  <LI>Constants, on the other hand, are safe. A constant, in Python, is =
a=20
  variable whose value is initialized but not changed. An example is=20
  <VAR>LABEL</VAR>, above.=20
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
<H2><A name=3DSECTION004540000000000000000>2.5.4 Packages</A> </H2>
<P>A package is a way to organize a number of modules together as a =
unit. Python=20
packages can also contain other packages.=20
<P>To give us an example to talk about, consider the follow package =
structure:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>package_example/
package_example/__init__.py
package_example/module1.py
package_example/module2.py
package_example/A.py
package_example/B.py
</PRE></DIV>
<P>And, here are the contents:=20
<P>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE># __init__.py

# Expose definitions from modules in this package.
from module1 import class1
from module2 import class2
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/__init__.txt"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/package_example/__init__.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE># module1.py

class class1:
    def __init__(self):
        self.description =3D 'class #1'
    def show(self):
        print self.description
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_module1.txt" =

type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/package_example/python_101_module1.py</SPAN>).</A> =

</DIV></DIV>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE># module2.py

class class2:
    def __init__(self):
        self.description =3D 'class #2'
    def show(self):
        print self.description
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_module2.txt" =

type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/package_example/python_101_module2.py</SPAN>).</A> =

</DIV></DIV>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE># A.py

import B
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_A.txt"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/package_example/python_101_A.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
<DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE># B.py

def function_b():
    print 'Hello from function_b'
</PRE>
<DIV class=3Dfooter><A=20
href=3D"http://www.rexx.com/~dkuhlman/python_101/python_101_B.txt"=20
type=3Dtext/plain>Download as text (original file name: <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>Examples/package_example/python_101_B.py</SPAN>).</A> =
</DIV></DIV>
<P>In order to be used as a Python package (e.g. so that modules can be =
imported=20
from it) a directory must contain a file whose name is <SPAN=20
class=3Dfile>__init__.py</SPAN>. The code in this module is evaluated =
the first=20
time a module is imported from the package.=20
<P>In order to import modules from a package, you may either add the =
package=20
directory to <CODE>sys.path</CODE> or, if the parent directory is on=20
<CODE>sys.path</CODE>, use dot-notation to explicitly specify the path. =
In our=20
example, you might use: "<TT class=3Dsamp>import =
package_example.module1</TT>".=20
<P>A module in a package can import another module from the same package =

directly without using the path to the package. For example, the module =
<TT=20
class=3Dmodule>A</TT> in our sample package <TT =
class=3Dmodule>package_example</TT>=20
can import module <TT class=3Dmodule>B</TT> in the same package with =
"<TT=20
class=3Dsamp>import B</TT>". Module <TT class=3Dmodule>A</TT> does =
<EM>not</EM> need=20
to use "<TT class=3Dsamp>import package_example.B</TT>".=20
<P>You can find additional information on packages at <A class=3Durl=20
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/essays/packages.html">http://www.python=
.org/doc/essays/packages.html</A>.=20

<P>Suggested techniques:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>In the <SPAN class=3Dfile>__init__.py</SPAN> file, import and make =
available=20
  objects defined in modules in the package. Our sample package <TT=20
  class=3Dmodule>package_example</TT> does this. Then, you can use "<TT=20
  class=3Dsamp>from package_example import *</TT>" to import the package =
and its=20
  contents. For example:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; from package_example import *
&gt;&gt;&gt; dir()
['__builtins__', '__doc__', '__file__', '__name__',
'atexit', 'class1', 'class2', 'module1', 'module2',
'readline', 'rlcompleter', 'sl', 'sys']
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; c1 =3D class1()
&gt;&gt;&gt; c2 =3D class2()
&gt;&gt;&gt; c1.show()
class #1
&gt;&gt;&gt; c2.show()
class #2
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>A few additional notes:=20
<P>
<UL>
  <LI>With Python 2.3, you can collect the modules in a package into a =
Zip file=20
  by using <TT class=3Dmodule>PyZipFile</TT> from the Python standard =
library. See=20
  <A class=3Durl=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/pyzipfile-objects.html">htt=
p://www.python.org/doc/current/lib/pyzipfile-objects.html</A>.=20

  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; import zipfile
&gt;&gt;&gt; a =3D zipfile.PyZipFile('mypackage.zip', 'w', =
zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED)
&gt;&gt;&gt; a.writepy('Examples')
&gt;&gt;&gt; a.close()
</PRE></DIV>
  <P>Then you can import and use this archive by inserting its path in=20
  <VAR>sys.path</VAR>. In the following example, <TT=20
  class=3Dmodule>class_basic_1</TT> is a module within package <TT=20
  class=3Dmodule>mypackage</TT>:=20
  <P>
  <DIV class=3Dverbatim><PRE>&gt;&gt;&gt; import sys
&gt;&gt;&gt; sys.path.insert(0, '/w2/Txt/Training/mypackage.zip')
&gt;&gt;&gt; import class_basic_1
Basic -- name: Apricot
&gt;&gt;&gt; obj =3D class_basic_1.Basic('Wilma')
&gt;&gt;&gt; obj.show()
Basic -- name: Wilma
</PRE></DIV>
  <P></P></LI></UL>
<P>
